## Proof

We evaluate the improper integral

$$I = \int_0^{\infty} e^{x^2}\,dx = \lim_{R\to\infty}\int_0^{R} e^{x^2}\,dx.$$

**Claim.** The integral diverges to $+\infty$.

**Proof.** The integrand $f(x)=e^{x^2}$ is continuous and strictly positive on $[0,\infty)$, so the partial integrals $F(R)=\int_0^R e^{x^2}\,dx$ are well-defined and strictly increasing in $R$. It suffices to show $F(R)\to+\infty$ as $R\to\infty$.

For $x\ge 1$, we have $x^2\ge x$, hence $e^{x^2}\ge e^x$. Therefore, for every $R\ge 1$,

$$\int_0^R e^{x^2}\,dx \;\ge\; \int_1^R e^{x^2}\,dx \;\ge\; \int_1^R e^x\,dx \;=\; e^R - e.$$

Since $e^R - e \to +\infty$ as $R\to\infty$, it follows that

$$\lim_{R\to\infty}\int_0^R e^{x^2}\,dx = +\infty.$$

Hence the improper integral diverges:

$$\int_0^{\infty} e^{x^2}\,dx = +\infty.$$

**Remark.** This is in direct contrast with the convergent Gaussian integral $\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2}\,dx=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}$, which converges precisely because the integrand decays. Here the sign in the exponent is positive, so the integrand grows super-exponentially and the area under the curve is unbounded.

